response_text:
In California, your landlord is very likely out of compliance if they waited **47 days** after you moved out to send deductions and return nothing.

Under **California Civil Code § 1950.5**, a landlord generally must, within **21 calendar days** after you move out:

1. Return your security deposit balance, **and**
2. Provide an itemized statement of deductions, **and**
3. Provide copies of receipts/invoices for deductions, unless a limited exception applies.

They can only deduct for things like unpaid rent, cleaning to return the unit to the same cleanliness as move-in, and repairs for damage **beyond ordinary wear and tear**. Routine repainting or normal scuffs/fading generally should not be charged to you, especially if the walls were fine and you have photos.

## What you can do now

### 1. Send a written demand letter immediately
Send it by **certified mail** and also by email/text if you have those channels. Keep copies.

Demand return of the full **$3,200** because:
- They missed the **21-day deadline**;
- The claimed damages are disputed and not supported;
- The $850 painting charge appears to be ordinary wear and tear/routine turnover;
- You have move-out photos showing the unit was clean and undamaged;
- They failed to timely provide a proper accounting/receipts.

Give them a short deadline, such as **7–10 days**, to return the money.

### Sample demand language

> Dear [Landlord/Property Manager],  
>   
> I moved out of [address] on [date]. Under California Civil Code § 1950.5, you were required to return my security deposit and/or provide a proper itemized statement with supporting documentation within 21 calendar days. I did not receive your claimed deductions until 47 days after move-out.  
>   
> I dispute all deductions, including the $850 painting charge. The unit was left clean and undamaged, and I have move-out photos documenting its condition. Routine painting and ordinary wear and tear are not valid security deposit deductions under California law.  
>   
> Please return my full security deposit of $3,200 within 10 days of this letter. If you do not, I am prepared to file a claim in small claims court for the deposit, plus statutory damages for bad-faith withholding of up to twice the deposit amount under Civil Code § 1950.5, court costs, and any other available relief.  
>   
> Sincerely,  
> [Your name]

## 2. Ask for receipts/invoices
If they claim actual costs, request:
- Contractor invoices;
- Receipts;
- Photos they relied on;
- Move-in condition report;
- Painting date before you moved in;
- Explanation of why painting was not ordinary wear and tear.

For deductions over a small threshold, California generally requires documentation, not just vague estimates.

## 3. Prepare for small claims court
If they don’t refund you, you can sue in **California small claims court**.

You can typically ask for:
- The **$3,200 deposit**;
- Potential **bad-faith damages up to 2x the deposit**, meaning up to **$6,400 additional**;
- Court filing/service costs.

So your potential claim could be up to **$9,600**, which is within California small claims limits for an individual plaintiff.

Bring:
- Lease;
- Deposit receipt/proof of payment;
- Move-out photos/videos;
- Any move-in photos;
- Texts/emails about move-out;
- Their late itemized list;
- Envelope/postmark or proof of when it was sent/received;
- Your demand letter and proof of mailing;
- Witness statements if anyone saw the condition.

## 4. Don’t ignore the timing issue
The **47-day delay is important**. Even if a landlord claims damages, missing the 21-day deadline and withholding the entire deposit can support an argument that the withholding was improper or in bad faith.

## 5. Consider local tenant help
Depending on your city/county, there may be a tenants’ rights organization or housing department that can help draft the letter or mediate.

Bottom line: send a firm written demand now. If they don’t return the deposit, small claims court is a practical next step, and your photos plus their late accounting put you in a strong position.
